InsectaG  &  InsectaS
https://zenodo.org/records/13858306 The original documentation does not include a description of the 
all_string_dnas field, it directly contains the DNA strings:
load('insect_dataset.mat'); 
% all_string_dnas stores all the sequences
Starting from the same dataset, we use two protocols with different sequences and different labels: 
one based on genera, the InsectaG dataset, and one based on species, InsectaS. 
Below is the code to recreate the two different testing protocols:

%InsectaS - species
test = [test_seen_loc];%In this vector, we store the indices of the patterns belonging to the test set 
train = [train_loc val_seen_loc val_unseen_loc];%it contains the indices for the training and validation sets. Therefore, if you need a validation set, 
%check and extract the indices corresponding to the two val* subsets
idTR{fold}=train;
idTE{fold}=test;
%Using these indices, you can then extract the sequences from the all_string_dnas structure and subsequently compute the descriptors needed to train a neural network.

labelTR{fold}=all_labels(train);
labelTE{fold}=all_labels(test);
%At the species level:
%CNNs have issues when some classes are missing, due to the number of output neurons. 
%Therefore, the labels must be modified to ensure they are contiguous.
lab=unique(labelTR{1});
for newLb=1:length(lab)
    NewLABtr(find(labelTR{1}==lab(newLb)))=newLb;%training labels
end
labelTR=NewLABtr;
for newLb=1:length(lab)
    NewLABte(find(labelTE{1}==lab(newLb)))=newLb;%test labels
end
%now you can train/test your approach using the labels stored in NewLABtr & NewLABte

%InsectaG - Genus
test = [test_unseen_loc];%In this vector, we store the indices of the patterns belonging to the test set
%As you can see, the test set is different in InsectaS and InsectaG
labelTRgenus{fold}=species2genus(labelTR{fold});%training labels
labelTEgenus{fold}=species2genus(all_labels(test));%test labels






